    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "remove the multi-lingual support and keep only the English language interface for the time being.  I\'m interested in a generic app that allows me to provide a topic or something i want to gather my thoughts about and helps me organize those ideas into structured outlines or lists. it should provide different ways to structure the questions or ways it askes about something that might be easier to say a lot or think through complex concepts more efficiently.  sometimes its hard to see the big picture when everything is just scattered thoughts.\n\nHere is something to consider, \"# Extraction \342\200\224 Design Doc\n\nA conversational interview app for surfacing what\'s in your head: ideas, feelings, loose opinions, half-formed thoughts. Where concordance *narrows down* a known set of options, this app *generates* the set in the first place through structured questioning \342\200\224 then optionally hands it off to concordance\'s ranking modes.\n\n---\n\n## The core problem\n\nJournaling is hard to start. Talking out loud to yourself is weird. Therapy is expensive. AI chat is shapeless. Most tools that try to \"get stuff out of your head\" either:\n\n- Dump you into a blank box (journal), which requires too much already knowing what to say\n- Ask generic questions that don\'t respond to what you\'re actually saying\n- Require verbal fluency when your thoughts aren\'t verbal yet\n\nThis app\'s job is to *meet you where the thought actually lives* \342\200\224 which is sometimes in words, sometimes in a gut feeling, sometimes in a vague image, sometimes in a comparison between two framings. Different modes unlock different cognitive registers.\n\n---\n\n## Session flow\n\n```\n1. Topic intake        \342\206\222 What is this session about? (freeform or prompted)\n2. Intention setting   \342\206\222 What do you want to get out of this?\n3. Mode selection      \342\206\222 System suggests; you choose or override\n4. Extraction          \342\206\222 One or more modes run; thoughts accumulate\n5. Review & edit       \342\206\222 See what came out; cull noise, highlight signals\n6. Concordance pass    \342\206\222 Optional: hand extracted items to concordance for ranking/sorting\n7. Export              \342\206\222 Markdown summary, shareable link, email to self\n```\n\nEach stage is escapable. You can skip straight to extraction, mid-session switch modes, or end early and export whatever you have.\n\n---\n\n## Mode selection logic\n\nBefore jumping into a mode, a few warm-up questions narrow the recommendation:\n\n| Prompt | Signals |\n|---|---|\n| \"Do you feel clear or foggy about this?\" | Clear \342\206\222 structured modes; Foggy \342\206\222 free/associative modes |\n| \"Is this more emotional or analytical?\" | Emotional \342\206\222 image, letter, finish-the-sentence; Analytical \342\206\222 drill, bracket, slider |\n| \"How much time do you have?\" | < 5 min \342\206\222 quick fire / swipe; > 20 min \342\206\222 guided drill / card sort |\n| \"Are you trying to decide, process, or just capture?\" | Decide \342\206\222 bracket / priority; Process \342\206\222 letter / timeline; Capture \342\206\222 free stream / quick fire |\n\nThe system presents a recommended mode with a one-line rationale, but every other mode is always accessible. Users who already know what they want can skip selection entirely.\n\n---\n\n## Extraction modes\n\n### 1. Free Stream\n**What it is:** Blank input, no prompts. Just go. Text or voice.\n\n**When it works:** When you already know roughly what you want to say and just need somewhere to put it. Good warm-up before any other mode. Good cooldown to summarize.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Voice \342\206\222 transcription (client-side or API)\n- Optional: after a pause of N seconds, system offers a single lightweight prompt: \"What else?\" or \"What are you not saying?\"\n- No other AI interruption during free stream \342\200\224 that defeats the purpose\n\n---\n\n### 2. Quick Fire\n**What it is:** Rapid-fire one-at-a-time prompts; you give 1\342\200\2235 word answers. The goal is volume and speed, not depth.\n\n**When it works:** When you\'re stuck, blanked out, or want to unstick. When you know there\'s *something* there but can\'t access it. Also useful early in a session to generate raw material before going deeper.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- System generates 20\342\200\22330 prompts tuned to the topic (AI-generated, or curated by topic type)\n- Prompts cycle automatically every few seconds or on tap\n- You skip what doesn\'t land; answer what does\n- Answers are 1\342\200\2237 words. Anything longer triggers a \"save that for later\" nudge\n\n**Example prompt sequences (topic: \"should I change jobs\"):**\n- \"What word comes up when you think about Monday morning?\"\n- \"Who are you jealous of right now?\"\n- \"What would you do if money wasn\'t the question?\"\n- \"What are you pretending is fine?\"\n\n---\n\n### 3. Guided Drill\n**What it is:** Conversational interview. Each answer shapes the next question. Slowest, deepest mode.\n\n**When it works:** When you have time and want to actually think something through. When there\'s a real decision or emotion underneath the topic.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- AI asks one question at a time\n- Classic follow-up patterns: \"Why does that matter?\", \"Say more about X\", \"What would have to be true for that to change?\"\n- System tracks threads \342\200\224 if you mention something interesting and move on, it can come back to it: \"Earlier you said X. Want to come back to that?\"\n- Pace is user-controlled: skip, expand, or end the drill any time\n\n**Anti-pattern to avoid:** Not every thread needs the 5-whys treatment. System should recognize when a thought is resolved and move on.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Binary Frame / Bracket\n**What it is:** System presents two framings of the same situation; you pick which feels more true.\n\n**When it works:** When you\'re pulled between two interpretations and can\'t figure out which one is actually driving you. When your thinking keeps going in circles.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- \"Which feels more true: [A] or [B]?\"\n- Both options are offered with an \"or something else entirely\" escape\n- Winning frame leads to the next round of choices \342\200\224 brackets collapse toward a core belief\n- The \"items\" being bracketed are *interpretations*, not options (unlike concordance where items are concrete choices)\n\n**Example (topic: \"I keep putting off the conversation with my manager\"):**\n- \"Which feels more true: *I\'m scared of their reaction* or *I don\'t actually think it\'ll help*?\"\n- \342\206\222 \"I\'m scared of their reaction\"\n- \"Which feels more true: *I\'m worried about conflict* or *I\'m worried about being dismissed*?\"\n\n---\n\n### 5. Swipe / React\n**What it is:** System presents candidate statements one at a time. You swipe or tap: resonates / doesn\'t / maybe.\n\n**When it works:** When you need to validate or calibrate \342\200\224 you\'ve got a sense of things but want to check what\'s actually load-bearing vs noise. Also useful after guided drill or quick fire: system generates candidate statements from your answers, you confirm which ones hit.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Keyboard: \342\206\220 / \342\206\222 / \342\206\223 (like concordance swipe)\n- After any session, system can synthesize candidate statements for a swipe pass\n- \"Maybe\" pile gets a second pass at the end\n- Mirror mode: system shows you what you said; you confirm it actually reflects what you meant\n\n---\n\n### 6. Slider / Intensity Map\n**What it is:** For each thought or statement, rate on a dimension.\n\n**When it works:** When you need to surface *how much* rather than *what*. Good for surfacing hidden intensities \342\200\224 things you say casually that are actually load-bearing.\n\n**Dimensions available (user picks 1\342\200\2232):**\n- Urgency: how time-sensitive is this? (now \342\206\222 eventually)\n- Certainty: how sure are you? (gut feeling \342\206\222 confident)\n- Emotion: how charged is this? (neutral \342\206\222 very charged)\n- Willingness to act: would you actually do something about this? (no \342\206\222 yes)\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- After quick fire or free stream, the extracted thoughts get run through a slider pass\n- One slider per thought\n- Results visualize as a heat map \342\200\224 which thoughts score high on urgency *and* certainty are the ones to act on\n\n---\n\n### 7. Card Sort / Cluster\n**What it is:** Spatial drag-and-drop. Thoughts appear as cards; you pull them into groups you name yourself.\n\n**When it works:** When you\'ve generated a lot of raw material and need to see structure. Good for identifying themes or separating concerns.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Cards generated from any previous mode\'s output\n- You create named buckets (themes) and drag cards in\n- System can suggest bucket names based on patterns, but you control taxonomy\n- Uncategorized cards stay in a \"loose\" pile visible the whole time\n\n**Connection to concordance:** This is essentially concordance\'s tier mode but with user-defined tiers and thought-fragments instead of pre-loaded items.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Timeline\n**What it is:** Drag thoughts/events onto a horizontal timeline: past \342\206\222 present \342\206\222 future.\n\n**When it works:** When a topic has temporal structure \342\200\224 something that happened, how you feel now, what you want or fear ahead.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Three zones: Before / Now / After (or more fine-grained: months ago / last week / today / this week / next month / someday)\n- Drag thoughts from a sidebar onto the timeline\n- Cards can stack at the same time point \342\200\224 visual density shows where the weight is\n- Good reveal: if everything you said is in the \"past\" zone, the problem may be unprocessed history, not current decision\n\n---\n\n### 9. Stakeholder Map\n**What it is:** Spatial placement of people (or groups) involved in the topic, by proximity and influence.\n\n**When it works:** When the topic involves relationships, dynamics, or \"who\'s part of this.\"\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Concentric circles: center = yourself; inner ring = close/direct; outer = peripheral\n- Drag names into position\n- Optional second axis: supported / opposed (for decisions that involve buy-in)\n- Seeing it laid out often reveals who you\'re not thinking about, or who you\'re over-weighting\n\n---\n\n### 10. Image / Visual Association\n**What it is:** A curated deck of images appears. You tap the ones that resonate with your topic or feeling.\n\n**When it works:** When the feeling is pre-verbal \342\200\224 you know something is there but words keep landing wrong. Visual stimuli bypass the verbal self-editor.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Decks organized by theme: textures, environments, faces (non-specific), objects, abstract shapes, colors\n- You select any that feel *somehow relevant* \342\200\224 no justification needed yet\n- After selection, system asks: \"What do these have in common?\" \342\200\224 that\'s the actual extraction\n- User-uploadable images for personal decks (places, people, things from your actual life)\n\n---\n\n### 11. Finish the Sentence\n**What it is:** Structured incomplete prompts. You complete them.\n\n**When it works:** When you\'re avoiding saying the thing. These prompts create a runway that lands you somewhere you wouldn\'t have gone alone.\n\n**Example prompts (topic-tuned):**\n- \"The thing I keep not saying is...\"\n- \"If I\'m honest, what I actually want is...\"\n- \"I think I\'m more [X] than I\'m admitting because...\"\n- \"The version of me I want to be would...\"\n- \"The real question isn\'t X, it\'s...\"\n- \"If I could tell [person] one thing without consequences, it would be...\"\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- 3\342\200\2237 prompts per session, tuned to topic type\n- You can skip any\n- Answers fed into the broader extraction, treated like any other captured thought\n\n---\n\n### 12. Devil\'s Advocate\n**What it is:** System presents counter-arguments or uncomfortable alternative readings. You respond.\n\n**When it works:** When you want to pressure-test an opinion before sharing it or acting on it. Good for decisions you\'ve half-made that feel shaky.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- System generates 3\342\200\2235 specific challenges: \"Someone might say X \342\200\224 what do you make of that?\"\n- Or: \"The strongest argument *against* your current position is Y. What does that bring up?\"\n- Your responses capture what you actually believe when pushed\n\n---\n\n### 13. Letter Writing\n**What it is:** Write (or voice) a letter \342\200\224 to someone involved, to yourself, or to the topic itself.\n\n**When it works:** Unlocks emotional processing mode. Especially good for topics involving relationships, regret, grief, or transition. Gives permission to be honest in a way a \"what do you think?\" prompt doesn\'t.\n\n**Frames available:**\n- Letter to a person (named)\n- Letter to yourself at a future point: \"Dear me in one year...\"\n- Letter from yourself at a past point: \"What would past-me want present-me to know?\"\n- Letter to the situation: \"Dear [job / relationship / version of this plan]...\"\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Full free-form input\n- System doesn\'t interrupt\n- After completion, optional: \"Is there anything you said there that surprised you?\"\n\n---\n\n### 14. Priority Pile\n**What it is:** Given everything extracted so far, sort thoughts into urgency tiers.\n\n**When it works:** At the end of a session when you have a pile of thoughts and need to figure out what to actually do.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Three buckets: *Act on this* / *Worth watching* / *Good to know, leave it*\n- Or a 2x2: Urgent \303\227 Important (Eisenhower matrix)\n- All extracted thoughts from the session appear as cards\n- After sorting, \"Act on this\" pile becomes the action item list you export\n\n---\n\n## The concordance handoff\n\nAfter extraction, any session\'s outputs can be pushed directly into concordance as a new set:\n\n- Each captured thought becomes a concordance `Item`\n- User picks a mode: Swipe to mark what matters, Bracket to find the single most important thing, Tier to categorize, Rank to order\n- This bridges \"surfacing\" (this app) with \"narrowing\" (concordance) \342\200\224 they\'re complementary pipeline stages\n\n```\nExtract \342\206\222 Review \342\206\222 [optional concordance pass] \342\206\222 Export\n```\n\nThe concordance pass is especially useful when you\'ve surfaced 15+ thoughts and need to figure out which 3 you actually care about.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-modal sessions\n\nUsers aren\'t locked to one mode per session. Suggested patterns:\n\n| Pattern | Modes | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warm-up \342\206\222 depth | Quick Fire \342\206\222 Guided Drill | Stuck, foggy, need to unstick |\n| Capture \342\206\222 sort | Free Stream \342\206\222 Card Sort | Know what you want to say, need structure |\n| Visual \342\206\222 verbal | Image Association \342\206\222 Finish the Sentence | Pre-verbal feelings, emotional topic |\n| Generate \342\206\222 validate | Free Stream \342\206\222 Swipe | Want to check what\'s actually load-bearing |\n| Decide | Binary Bracket \342\206\222 Priority Pile | Decision that keeps circling |\n| Process | Letter \342\206\222 Guided Drill | Relationship or transition topic |\n\nSessions can also be sequential across days: return to an open session, add to it, see how the extracted thoughts feel different after sleeping on them.\n\n---\n\n## Output / export\n\nAt the end of any session:\n\n- **Markdown summary** \342\200\224 date, topic, mode(s) used, captured thoughts (bulleted), any highlights you marked\n- **Action items only** \342\200\224 just the priority-pile \"act on this\" pile\n- **Email to self** \342\200\224 for the thoughts you need to not lose\n- **Push to concordance** \342\200\224 create a new set from this session\'s thoughts\n- **Shareable link** \342\200\224 base64-encoded snapshot, readable without an account (same model as concordance Stage 3)\n\n---\n\n## What this is not\n\n- Not a productivity system. It doesn\'t manage tasks or remind you of things.\n- Not therapy. It surfaces; it doesn\'t interpret or diagnose.\n- Not a journal. Sessions are structured and time-bounded. The point is extraction, not ongoing reflection.\n- Not a chatbot. The AI serves the interaction design \342\200\224 it generates prompts and candidate statements \342\200\224 but it shouldn\'t feel like talking to an assistant.\n\n---\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **Session persistence.** Should open sessions auto-save and be resumable? (Probably yes, same localStorage model as concordance.)\n- **Privacy model.** Some of what gets surfaced here is sensitive. Local-first by default; no server unless user opts in. Voice data especially should never leave the device without explicit consent.\n- **Mode discovery.** How do new users find modes they didn\'t know to look for? Onboarding tour? Inline \"try a different approach\" nudge during sessions?\n- **AI dependency.** Some modes (quick fire, guided drill, candidate statement generation for swipe) need AI. Others (free stream, card sort, timeline, stakeholder map, image association) are fully offline. Worth being explicit about which modes need a key and which don\'t.\n- **Topic types.** \"Should I change jobs\" vs \"how do I feel about this relationship\" vs \"what do I actually think about this idea\" all call for different prompt decks. Worth building a topic taxonomy to tune the AI prompts.\n\""
    items {
      text: "remove the multi-lingual support and keep only the English language interface for the time being.  I\'m interested in a generic app that allows me to provide a topic or something i want to gather my thoughts about and helps me organize those ideas into structured outlines or lists. it should provide different ways to structure the questions or ways it askes about something that might be easier to say a lot or think through complex concepts more efficiently.  sometimes its hard to see the big picture when everything is just scattered thoughts.\n\nHere is something to consider, \"# Extraction \342\200\224 Design Doc\n\nA conversational interview app for surfacing what\'s in your head: ideas, feelings, loose opinions, half-formed thoughts. Where concordance *narrows down* a known set of options, this app *generates* the set in the first place through structured questioning \342\200\224 then optionally hands it off to concordance\'s ranking modes.\n\n---\n\n## The core problem\n\nJournaling is hard to start. Talking out loud to yourself is weird. Therapy is expensive. AI chat is shapeless. Most tools that try to \"get stuff out of your head\" either:\n\n- Dump you into a blank box (journal), which requires too much already knowing what to say\n- Ask generic questions that don\'t respond to what you\'re actually saying\n- Require verbal fluency when your thoughts aren\'t verbal yet\n\nThis app\'s job is to *meet you where the thought actually lives* \342\200\224 which is sometimes in words, sometimes in a gut feeling, sometimes in a vague image, sometimes in a comparison between two framings. Different modes unlock different cognitive registers.\n\n---\n\n## Session flow\n\n```\n1. Topic intake        \342\206\222 What is this session about? (freeform or prompted)\n2. Intention setting   \342\206\222 What do you want to get out of this?\n3. Mode selection      \342\206\222 System suggests; you choose or override\n4. Extraction          \342\206\222 One or more modes run; thoughts accumulate\n5. Review & edit       \342\206\222 See what came out; cull noise, highlight signals\n6. Concordance pass    \342\206\222 Optional: hand extracted items to concordance for ranking/sorting\n7. Export              \342\206\222 Markdown summary, shareable link, email to self\n```\n\nEach stage is escapable. You can skip straight to extraction, mid-session switch modes, or end early and export whatever you have.\n\n---\n\n## Mode selection logic\n\nBefore jumping into a mode, a few warm-up questions narrow the recommendation:\n\n| Prompt | Signals |\n|---|---|\n| \"Do you feel clear or foggy about this?\" | Clear \342\206\222 structured modes; Foggy \342\206\222 free/associative modes |\n| \"Is this more emotional or analytical?\" | Emotional \342\206\222 image, letter, finish-the-sentence; Analytical \342\206\222 drill, bracket, slider |\n| \"How much time do you have?\" | < 5 min \342\206\222 quick fire / swipe; > 20 min \342\206\222 guided drill / card sort |\n| \"Are you trying to decide, process, or just capture?\" | Decide \342\206\222 bracket / priority; Process \342\206\222 letter / timeline; Capture \342\206\222 free stream / quick fire |\n\nThe system presents a recommended mode with a one-line rationale, but every other mode is always accessible. Users who already know what they want can skip selection entirely.\n\n---\n\n## Extraction modes\n\n### 1. Free Stream\n**What it is:** Blank input, no prompts. Just go. Text or voice.\n\n**When it works:** When you already know roughly what you want to say and just need somewhere to put it. Good warm-up before any other mode. Good cooldown to summarize.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Voice \342\206\222 transcription (client-side or API)\n- Optional: after a pause of N seconds, system offers a single lightweight prompt: \"What else?\" or \"What are you not saying?\"\n- No other AI interruption during free stream \342\200\224 that defeats the purpose\n\n---\n\n### 2. Quick Fire\n**What it is:** Rapid-fire one-at-a-time prompts; you give 1\342\200\2235 word answers. The goal is volume and speed, not depth.\n\n**When it works:** When you\'re stuck, blanked out, or want to unstick. When you know there\'s *something* there but can\'t access it. Also useful early in a session to generate raw material before going deeper.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- System generates 20\342\200\22330 prompts tuned to the topic (AI-generated, or curated by topic type)\n- Prompts cycle automatically every few seconds or on tap\n- You skip what doesn\'t land; answer what does\n- Answers are 1\342\200\2237 words. Anything longer triggers a \"save that for later\" nudge\n\n**Example prompt sequences (topic: \"should I change jobs\"):**\n- \"What word comes up when you think about Monday morning?\"\n- \"Who are you jealous of right now?\"\n- \"What would you do if money wasn\'t the question?\"\n- \"What are you pretending is fine?\"\n\n---\n\n### 3. Guided Drill\n**What it is:** Conversational interview. Each answer shapes the next question. Slowest, deepest mode.\n\n**When it works:** When you have time and want to actually think something through. When there\'s a real decision or emotion underneath the topic.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- AI asks one question at a time\n- Classic follow-up patterns: \"Why does that matter?\", \"Say more about X\", \"What would have to be true for that to change?\"\n- System tracks threads \342\200\224 if you mention something interesting and move on, it can come back to it: \"Earlier you said X. Want to come back to that?\"\n- Pace is user-controlled: skip, expand, or end the drill any time\n\n**Anti-pattern to avoid:** Not every thread needs the 5-whys treatment. System should recognize when a thought is resolved and move on.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Binary Frame / Bracket\n**What it is:** System presents two framings of the same situation; you pick which feels more true.\n\n**When it works:** When you\'re pulled between two interpretations and can\'t figure out which one is actually driving you. When your thinking keeps going in circles.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- \"Which feels more true: [A] or [B]?\"\n- Both options are offered with an \"or something else entirely\" escape\n- Winning frame leads to the next round of choices \342\200\224 brackets collapse toward a core belief\n- The \"items\" being bracketed are *interpretations*, not options (unlike concordance where items are concrete choices)\n\n**Example (topic: \"I keep putting off the conversation with my manager\"):**\n- \"Which feels more true: *I\'m scared of their reaction* or *I don\'t actually think it\'ll help*?\"\n- \342\206\222 \"I\'m scared of their reaction\"\n- \"Which feels more true: *I\'m worried about conflict* or *I\'m worried about being dismissed*?\"\n\n---\n\n### 5. Swipe / React\n**What it is:** System presents candidate statements one at a time. You swipe or tap: resonates / doesn\'t / maybe.\n\n**When it works:** When you need to validate or calibrate \342\200\224 you\'ve got a sense of things but want to check what\'s actually load-bearing vs noise. Also useful after guided drill or quick fire: system generates candidate statements from your answers, you confirm which ones hit.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Keyboard: \342\206\220 / \342\206\222 / \342\206\223 (like concordance swipe)\n- After any session, system can synthesize candidate statements for a swipe pass\n- \"Maybe\" pile gets a second pass at the end\n- Mirror mode: system shows you what you said; you confirm it actually reflects what you meant\n\n---\n\n### 6. Slider / Intensity Map\n**What it is:** For each thought or statement, rate on a dimension.\n\n**When it works:** When you need to surface *how much* rather than *what*. Good for surfacing hidden intensities \342\200\224 things you say casually that are actually load-bearing.\n\n**Dimensions available (user picks 1\342\200\2232):**\n- Urgency: how time-sensitive is this? (now \342\206\222 eventually)\n- Certainty: how sure are you? (gut feeling \342\206\222 confident)\n- Emotion: how charged is this? (neutral \342\206\222 very charged)\n- Willingness to act: would you actually do something about this? (no \342\206\222 yes)\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- After quick fire or free stream, the extracted thoughts get run through a slider pass\n- One slider per thought\n- Results visualize as a heat map \342\200\224 which thoughts score high on urgency *and* certainty are the ones to act on\n\n---\n\n### 7. Card Sort / Cluster\n**What it is:** Spatial drag-and-drop. Thoughts appear as cards; you pull them into groups you name yourself.\n\n**When it works:** When you\'ve generated a lot of raw material and need to see structure. Good for identifying themes or separating concerns.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Cards generated from any previous mode\'s output\n- You create named buckets (themes) and drag cards in\n- System can suggest bucket names based on patterns, but you control taxonomy\n- Uncategorized cards stay in a \"loose\" pile visible the whole time\n\n**Connection to concordance:** This is essentially concordance\'s tier mode but with user-defined tiers and thought-fragments instead of pre-loaded items.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Timeline\n**What it is:** Drag thoughts/events onto a horizontal timeline: past \342\206\222 present \342\206\222 future.\n\n**When it works:** When a topic has temporal structure \342\200\224 something that happened, how you feel now, what you want or fear ahead.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Three zones: Before / Now / After (or more fine-grained: months ago / last week / today / this week / next month / someday)\n- Drag thoughts from a sidebar onto the timeline\n- Cards can stack at the same time point \342\200\224 visual density shows where the weight is\n- Good reveal: if everything you said is in the \"past\" zone, the problem may be unprocessed history, not current decision\n\n---\n\n### 9. Stakeholder Map\n**What it is:** Spatial placement of people (or groups) involved in the topic, by proximity and influence.\n\n**When it works:** When the topic involves relationships, dynamics, or \"who\'s part of this.\"\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Concentric circles: center = yourself; inner ring = close/direct; outer = peripheral\n- Drag names into position\n- Optional second axis: supported / opposed (for decisions that involve buy-in)\n- Seeing it laid out often reveals who you\'re not thinking about, or who you\'re over-weighting\n\n---\n\n### 10. Image / Visual Association\n**What it is:** A curated deck of images appears. You tap the ones that resonate with your topic or feeling.\n\n**When it works:** When the feeling is pre-verbal \342\200\224 you know something is there but words keep landing wrong. Visual stimuli bypass the verbal self-editor.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Decks organized by theme: textures, environments, faces (non-specific), objects, abstract shapes, colors\n- You select any that feel *somehow relevant* \342\200\224 no justification needed yet\n- After selection, system asks: \"What do these have in common?\" \342\200\224 that\'s the actual extraction\n- User-uploadable images for personal decks (places, people, things from your actual life)\n\n---\n\n### 11. Finish the Sentence\n**What it is:** Structured incomplete prompts. You complete them.\n\n**When it works:** When you\'re avoiding saying the thing. These prompts create a runway that lands you somewhere you wouldn\'t have gone alone.\n\n**Example prompts (topic-tuned):**\n- \"The thing I keep not saying is...\"\n- \"If I\'m honest, what I actually want is...\"\n- \"I think I\'m more [X] than I\'m admitting because...\"\n- \"The version of me I want to be would...\"\n- \"The real question isn\'t X, it\'s...\"\n- \"If I could tell [person] one thing without consequences, it would be...\"\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- 3\342\200\2237 prompts per session, tuned to topic type\n- You can skip any\n- Answers fed into the broader extraction, treated like any other captured thought\n\n---\n\n### 12. Devil\'s Advocate\n**What it is:** System presents counter-arguments or uncomfortable alternative readings. You respond.\n\n**When it works:** When you want to pressure-test an opinion before sharing it or acting on it. Good for decisions you\'ve half-made that feel shaky.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- System generates 3\342\200\2235 specific challenges: \"Someone might say X \342\200\224 what do you make of that?\"\n- Or: \"The strongest argument *against* your current position is Y. What does that bring up?\"\n- Your responses capture what you actually believe when pushed\n\n---\n\n### 13. Letter Writing\n**What it is:** Write (or voice) a letter \342\200\224 to someone involved, to yourself, or to the topic itself.\n\n**When it works:** Unlocks emotional processing mode. Especially good for topics involving relationships, regret, grief, or transition. Gives permission to be honest in a way a \"what do you think?\" prompt doesn\'t.\n\n**Frames available:**\n- Letter to a person (named)\n- Letter to yourself at a future point: \"Dear me in one year...\"\n- Letter from yourself at a past point: \"What would past-me want present-me to know?\"\n- Letter to the situation: \"Dear [job / relationship / version of this plan]...\"\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Full free-form input\n- System doesn\'t interrupt\n- After completion, optional: \"Is there anything you said there that surprised you?\"\n\n---\n\n### 14. Priority Pile\n**What it is:** Given everything extracted so far, sort thoughts into urgency tiers.\n\n**When it works:** At the end of a session when you have a pile of thoughts and need to figure out what to actually do.\n\n**Mechanics:**\n- Three buckets: *Act on this* / *Worth watching* / *Good to know, leave it*\n- Or a 2x2: Urgent \303\227 Important (Eisenhower matrix)\n- All extracted thoughts from the session appear as cards\n- After sorting, \"Act on this\" pile becomes the action item list you export\n\n---\n\n## The concordance handoff\n\nAfter extraction, any session\'s outputs can be pushed directly into concordance as a new set:\n\n- Each captured thought becomes a concordance `Item`\n- User picks a mode: Swipe to mark what matters, Bracket to find the single most important thing, Tier to categorize, Rank to order\n- This bridges \"surfacing\" (this app) with \"narrowing\" (concordance) \342\200\224 they\'re complementary pipeline stages\n\n```\nExtract \342\206\222 Review \342\206\222 [optional concordance pass] \342\206\222 Export\n```\n\nThe concordance pass is especially useful when you\'ve surfaced 15+ thoughts and need to figure out which 3 you actually care about.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-modal sessions\n\nUsers aren\'t locked to one mode per session. Suggested patterns:\n\n| Pattern | Modes | When |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warm-up \342\206\222 depth | Quick Fire \342\206\222 Guided Drill | Stuck, foggy, need to unstick |\n| Capture \342\206\222 sort | Free Stream \342\206\222 Card Sort | Know what you want to say, need structure |\n| Visual \342\206\222 verbal | Image Association \342\206\222 Finish the Sentence | Pre-verbal feelings, emotional topic |\n| Generate \342\206\222 validate | Free Stream \342\206\222 Swipe | Want to check what\'s actually load-bearing |\n| Decide | Binary Bracket \342\206\222 Priority Pile | Decision that keeps circling |\n| Process | Letter \342\206\222 Guided Drill | Relationship or transition topic |\n\nSessions can also be sequential across days: return to an open session, add to it, see how the extracted thoughts feel different after sleeping on them.\n\n---\n\n## Output / export\n\nAt the end of any session:\n\n- **Markdown summary** \342\200\224 date, topic, mode(s) used, captured thoughts (bulleted), any highlights you marked\n- **Action items only** \342\200\224 just the priority-pile \"act on this\" pile\n- **Email to self** \342\200\224 for the thoughts you need to not lose\n- **Push to concordance** \342\200\224 create a new set from this session\'s thoughts\n- **Shareable link** \342\200\224 base64-encoded snapshot, readable without an account (same model as concordance Stage 3)\n\n---\n\n## What this is not\n\n- Not a productivity system. It doesn\'t manage tasks or remind you of things.\n- Not therapy. It surfaces; it doesn\'t interpret or diagnose.\n- Not a journal. Sessions are structured and time-bounded. The point is extraction, not ongoing reflection.\n- Not a chatbot. The AI serves the interaction design \342\200\224 it generates prompts and candidate statements \342\200\224 but it shouldn\'t feel like talking to an assistant.\n\n---\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **Session persistence.** Should open sessions auto-save and be resumable? (Probably yes, same localStorage model as concordance.)\n- **Privacy model.** Some of what gets surfaced here is sensitive. Local-first by default; no server unless user opts in. Voice data especially should never leave the device without explicit consent.\n- **Mode discovery.** How do new users find modes they didn\'t know to look for? Onboarding tour? Inline \"try a different approach\" nudge during sessions?\n- **AI dependency.** Some modes (quick fire, guided drill, candidate statement generation for swipe) need AI. Others (free stream, card sort, timeline, stakeholder map, image association) are fully offline. Worth being explicit about which modes need a key and which don\'t.\n- **Topic types.** \"Should I change jobs\" vs \"how do I feel about this relationship\" vs \"what do I actually think about this idea\" all call for different prompt decks. Worth building a topic taxonomy to tune the AI prompts.\n\""
    }
